---
import StandardPageLayout from "../../layouts/StandardPageLayout.astro";
import FinancialStatementKey from "../../components/FinancialStatementKey.astro";
import Glossary from "../../components/Glossary.astro";
import PageToc from "../../components/PageToc.astro";
import "../../styles/standards-poster.css";
import "../../styles/extended.css";
import HistoryTimeline from "../../components/HistoryTimeline.astro";
import history from "../../data/history.json";
import metadata from "../../data/standards/usgaap/asc910.json";
---

<StandardPageLayout
  code={metadata.code}
  standardType={metadata.framework}
  title={metadata.title}
  description={metadata.description}
  issued={metadata.issued}
  latestAmendment={metadata.latestAmendment}
  status={metadata.status}
  summary={metadata.summary}
  bannerNotice={metadata.bannerNotice}
>
  <Fragment slot="toc">
    <PageToc
      items={[
        { id: "s1", label: "1. Conceptual Framework Linkage" },
        { id: "s2", label: "2. Scope" },
        { id: "s3", label: "3. Contract Revenue Recognition — Over-Time via Input or Output Methods" },
        { id: "s4", label: "4. Contract Costs — What May Be Capitalised" },
        { id: "s5", label: "5. Combining and Segmenting Contracts" },
        { id: "s6", label: "6. Provision for Anticipated Losses on Contracts" },
        { id: "s7", label: "7. Contract Modifications, Claims, and Unpriced Change Orders" },
        { id: "s8", label: "8. Worked Example — Cost-to-Cost Input Method Revenue Calculation" },
        { id: "s9", label: "9. Worked Example — a Loss Contract Provision" },
        { id: "s10", label: "10. Disclosure Requirements" },
        { id: "s11", label: "11. Critical Judgments &amp; Estimates" },
        { id: "s12", label: "12. ASC 910 vs IFRS — Comprehensive Comparison" },
        { id: "s13", label: "13. Statement Mapping" },
        { id: "s14", label: "14. Related Topics" },
        { id: "s15", label: "15. Glossary" }
      ]}
    />
  </Fragment>
  <div class="wrap">

    <div class="section-header" id="s1"><span class="num">1</span><h2>Conceptual Framework Linkage</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card ci span2"><div class="ctitle ci">Industry-specific elaboration on ASC 606's general over-time model</div><div class="principle">Long-term construction contracts have long posed a distinctive accounting challenge: performance often spans MULTIPLE reporting periods, with revenue recognition needing to track PROGRESS toward completion rather than waiting for a single delivery event. ASC 910 layers CONSTRUCTION-INDUSTRY-SPECIFIC guidance — on contract costs, combining/segmenting contracts, and loss provisions — ON TOP of ASC 606's general over-time revenue recognition model, rather than establishing a wholly separate revenue framework of its own.</div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s2"><span class="num">2</span><h2>Scope</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cw span2"><div class="ctitle cw">Contractors performing long-term construction-type contracts</div><div class="principle">ASC 910 applies to entities performing construction-type contracts — including general contractors, homebuilders performing contract construction, engineering and design firms, and specialty subcontractors — where performance typically spans an extended period.</div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s3"><span class="num">3</span><h2>Contract Revenue Recognition — Over-Time via Input or Output Methods</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">The general ASC 606 over-time model, with construction-specific application guidance</div><div class="principle">Construction contracts commonly satisfy ASC 606's OVER-TIME recognition criteria (the customer typically controls the asset as it's created, or the contractor's performance creates an asset with no ALTERNATIVE USE and the contractor has an ENFORCEABLE right to payment for performance completed to date). Progress toward completion is measured using either an INPUT method (most commonly COST-TO-COST — costs incurred to date divided by total estimated contract costs) or an OUTPUT method (e.g. units delivered, surveys of work performed) — ASC 910 provides CONSTRUCTION-INDUSTRY-SPECIFIC guidance on applying these general ASC 606 methods.</div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s4"><span class="num">4</span><h2>Contract Costs — What May Be Capitalised</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cs span2"><div class="ctitle cs">Costs to fulfill vs costs to obtain a contract</div><div class="principle">Costs to FULFILL a contract — such as MOBILISATION costs (moving equipment/personnel to a job site) and UNINSTALLED MATERIALS delivered to a site but not yet incorporated into the project — may be CAPITALISED as contract assets (if they meet ASC 340-40's fulfillment cost criteria), rather than expensed as incurred, since they don't yet represent progress toward SATISFYING the performance obligation. Costs to OBTAIN a contract (e.g. sales commissions, bid/proposal costs) are separately evaluated under ASC 340-40's incremental cost of obtaining a contract guidance.</div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s5"><span class="num">5</span><h2>Combining and Segmenting Contracts</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cw span2"><div class="ctitle cw">Aligning the unit of account with economic substance</div><div class="principle">Multiple LEGALLY separate contracts entered into at or near the same time with the same customer may need to be COMBINED and accounted for as a single contract if they meet ASC 606's combination criteria (e.g. negotiated as a package with a single commercial objective, or consideration in one contract depends on the other). Conversely, a SINGLE contract covering genuinely distinct deliverables may be SEGMENTED into separate units of account if distinct performance obligations exist.</div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s6"><span class="num">6</span><h2>Provision for Anticipated Losses on Contracts</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">The ENTIRE anticipated loss is recognised immediately, regardless of percentage complete</div><div class="principle">When TOTAL estimated contract costs exceed TOTAL estimated contract revenue (an anticipated LOSS on the contract as a whole), the ENTIRE expected loss is recognised <strong>IMMEDIATELY</strong> in the period the loss becomes evident — NOT spread proportionally over the remaining contract term, and REGARDLESS of how much progress has been made to date. This "ANTICIPATED LOSS" provision requirement supplements ASC 606's general revenue guidance and continues to apply even after the shift to ASC 606's control-transfer model.</div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s7"><span class="num">7</span><h2>Contract Modifications, Claims, and Unpriced Change Orders</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cs span2"><div class="ctitle cs">Variable consideration and enforceability judgement</div><div class="principle">Construction contracts often involve CHANGE ORDERS (modifications to scope/price) and CLAIMS (amounts a contractor seeks to collect from a customer beyond the agreed contract price, e.g. for customer-caused delays) — these are evaluated under ASC 606's variable consideration and contract modification framework, requiring judgement about whether the additional consideration is ENFORCEABLE and can be included in the transaction price without significant risk of future reversal.</div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s8"><span class="num">8</span><h2>Worked Example — Cost-to-Cost Input Method Revenue Calculation</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cw span2"><div class="ctitle cw">Measuring progress toward completion</div>
    <div class="worked" style="margin-bottom:0;"><div class="wk-title">Example: A $20,000,000 highway construction contract</div><div class="wk-body">A contractor has a $20,000,000 contract with total estimated costs of $16,000,000. By year-end, $6,400,000 of costs have been incurred.</div><div class="wk-answer">Percentage complete = $6,400,000 ÷ $16,000,000 = <strong>40%</strong>. Revenue recognised to date = 40% × $20,000,000 = <strong>$8,000,000</strong>. Gross profit recognised to date = $8,000,000 − $6,400,000 = <strong>$1,600,000</strong>, reflecting progress measured using the cost-to-cost input method.</div></div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s9"><span class="num">9</span><h2>Worked Example — a Loss Contract Provision</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card ci span2"><div class="ctitle ci">Recognising the full loss immediately</div>
    <div class="worked" style="margin-bottom:0;"><div class="wk-title">Example: A contract whose total costs now exceed total revenue</div><div class="wk-body">A contractor's $10,000,000 fixed-price contract now has REVISED total estimated costs of $11,500,000 (up from an original estimate of $9,000,000, due to unexpected site conditions), with the contract 30% complete based on costs incurred to date ($3,450,000).</div><div class="wk-answer">Total anticipated LOSS on the contract = $10,000,000 − $11,500,000 = <strong>$1,500,000 loss</strong>. Despite the contract being only 30% complete, the ENTIRE $1,500,000 anticipated loss must be recognised <strong>IMMEDIATELY</strong> in the current period — not spread over the remaining 70% of the contract.</div></div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s10"><span class="num">10</span><h2>Disclosure Requirements</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">What ASC 910 requires (in addition to general ASC 606 disclosures)</div><div class="check-list"><span>Method used to measure progress toward completion for each type of contract</span><span>Basis for classifying assets/liabilities as current in a classified balance sheet, given the length of contract cycles</span><span>Details of revisions in contract estimates, including the effect of anticipated losses</span></div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s11"><span class="num">11</span><h2>Critical Judgments &amp; Estimates</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cn span2"><div class="ctitle cn">Where judgement matters most</div>
    <div class="two-col"><div><div class="elabel">SIGNIFICANT JUDGEMENTS</div><div class="dot-list"><span>Whether multiple contracts should be combined, or a single contract segmented</span><span>Enforceability of claims and unpriced change orders for inclusion in the transaction price</span></div></div>
    <div><div class="elabel">SIGNIFICANT ESTIMATES</div><div class="dot-list"><span>Total estimated contract costs, revised regularly as the contract progresses</span><span>Percentage of completion under the cost-to-cost or other input/output methods</span></div></div></div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s12"><span class="num">12</span><h2>ASC 910 vs IFRS — Comprehensive Comparison</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card ci span2"><div class="ctitle ci">Comparison — ASC 910 (with ASC 606) vs IFRS 15 for construction contracts</div>
    <div class="td-table"><div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1.2fr 1.2fr;"><span>Feature</span><span style="color:var(--color-text-warning);">US GAAP (ASC 910 / 606)</span><span style="color:var(--color-text-info);">IFRS (IFRS 15)</span></div>
    <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1.2fr 1.2fr;"><span>General revenue recognition model</span><span>Over-time, control-transfer based</span><span>Same — IAS 11 was superseded by IFRS 15, converging both frameworks on this same model</span></div>
    <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1.2fr 1.2fr;"><span>Progress measurement methods</span><span>Input (cost-to-cost) or output methods</span><span>Same input/output method categories — converged</span></div>
    <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1.2fr 1.2fr;"><span>Anticipated loss recognition</span><span>ENTIRE anticipated loss recognised immediately (ASC 910 supplementary guidance)</span><span>IAS 37's general ONEROUS CONTRACT model — provision at the LOWER of unavoidable costs and exit costs, a somewhat different measurement mechanic even though the practical outcome (recognising an expected loss) is broadly similar</span></div>
    </div>
    <div class="note">The headline revenue recognition MODEL is now genuinely converged (both frameworks moved to a single control-transfer standard), but the SUPPLEMENTARY loss provision mechanics retain a real, if narrower, difference — ASC 910's immediate full-loss approach vs IAS 37's unavoidable-cost/exit-cost measurement basis for onerous contracts.</div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s13"><span class="num">13</span><h2>Statement Mapping</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cs span2"><div class="ctitle cs">Where every ASC 910 event lands</div>
    <div class="td-table"><div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Event</span><span>Statement(s) affected</span><span>Notes</span></div>
    <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Contract revenue recognised over time</span><span><span class="badge bs">BS</span> <span class="badge bp">IS</span></span><span>Recognised as progress is made, per the cost-to-cost or other method</span></div>
    <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Anticipated loss provision</span><span><span class="badge bs">BS</span> <span class="badge bp">IS</span></span><span>Entire loss recognised immediately upon becoming evident</span></div>
    <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Capitalised fulfillment costs (mobilisation, uninstalled materials)</span><span><span class="badge bs">BS</span></span><span>Recognised as a contract asset until incorporated into progress</span></div>
    </div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s14"><span class="num">14</span><h2>Related Topics</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">Cross-reference map</div>
    <div class="td-table"><div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>Topic</span><span>Connection</span></div>
    <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>ASC 606</span><span>Supplies the general over-time revenue recognition model that ASC 910 elaborates on for construction contracts</span></div>
    <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>ASC 340-40</span><span>Supplies the framework for capitalising costs to fulfill and costs to obtain a contract</span></div>
    </div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s15"><span class="num">15</span><h2>Quick-Reference Glossary</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cn span2">
    <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Cost-to-cost method</div><div class="glossary-def">An input method measuring progress toward completion as costs incurred to date divided by total estimated contract costs.</div></div>
    <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Anticipated loss provision</div><div class="glossary-def">The requirement to recognise the entire estimated loss on a contract immediately, once total estimated costs exceed total estimated revenue.</div></div>
    <div class="glossary-item" style="border-bottom:none;"><div class="glossary-term">Uninstalled materials</div><div class="glossary-def">Materials delivered to a job site but not yet incorporated into the project, often excluded from progress measurement until installed.</div></div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <FinancialStatementKey />

  </div>
  <section id="history">
    <HistoryTimeline items={history.asc910 || []} />
  </section>
</StandardPageLayout>
